# W1 pins. version dist_date target sha256(.tar.xz)
# The key-pinning step APPENDS one line here of the form
#   # rust_release_key_fpr <40-hex>
# and `fetch-w1.sh --verify-key` enforces it on every fetch. The template
# must not itself contain a line matching that pattern — awk would match
# both and the guard would trip forever. CONFIRMED BY EXECUTION: a
# placeholder matching the pattern made awk emit two lines, the first
# being "<recorded".
# Each checksum is the pkg.rust.target.<target>.xz_hash field in
# https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/channel-rust-<version>.toml. Verify the
# manifest's .asc signature with GPG against the Rust release key pinned below.
# The 1.96.0 and 1.97.0 checksums were verified on 2026-08-10. The same
# procedure on 2026-08-11 reproduced both checksums and supplied the 1.95.0 pin.
#
# W1 key anchor decision: risk-accepted acquisition. No pre-existing
# anchor existed on this machine, so the fingerprint below was obtained by
# the bounded HTTPS fetch of rust-key.gpg.ascii from static.rust-lang.org on
# 2026-08-11. That is TRUST-ON-FIRST-USE, crossed exactly once; it is NOT
# corroboration. From here on it is a committed constant that
# `fetch-w1.sh --verify-key` enforces before every tarball fetch.
# rust_release_key_fpr 108F66205EAEB0AAA8DD5E1C85AB96E6FA1BE5FE
